---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PregnancyDueDateCalculator from '@/components/tools/health/PregnancyDueDateCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="pregnancy-due-date-calculator">
  <PregnancyDueDateCalculator client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the due date calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Pick the dating method you have the best information for. If you have had an early scan, use
        the ultrasound option. It is the most accurate.
      </li>
      <li>Enter the date. For the last-period method, also set your usual cycle length.</li>
      <li>
        Read your estimated due date, how many weeks and days pregnant you are today, and which
        trimester you are in.
      </li>
      <li>Scroll the milestone table for the dates of scans, tests and term windows.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Every date is calculated in your browser. Nothing you type is transmitted, stored or logged
      anywhere, and closing the tab discards it.
    </p>

    <h2>The four dating methods</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Method</th>
            <th scope="col">Calculation</th>
            <th scope="col">Accuracy</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Last menstrual period (Naegele's rule)</td>
            <td>LMP + 280 days, adjusted for cycle length</td>
            <td>±1–2 weeks; depends on regular cycles and an accurate recalled date</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Conception or ovulation date</td>
            <td>Conception + 266 days</td>
            <td>±3–5 days if ovulation was tracked properly</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>IVF transfer</td>
            <td>Transfer + 280 − 14 − embryo age in days</td>
            <td>The most precise of all. Embryo age is known exactly</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Dating ultrasound</td>
            <td>Scan date + (280 − gestational age at scan)</td>
            <td>±5 days in the first trimester, widening later in pregnancy</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Naegele's rule and the 28-day assumption</h3>
    <p>
      Franz Naegele's rule, published in 1812, adds 280 days to the first day of the last menstrual
      period. Buried inside it are two assumptions: that your cycle is 28 days, and that you ovulated
      on day 14. Neither holds for everybody. A 35-day cycle usually means ovulation around day 21, so
      the true due date is about a week later than the textbook rule gives. This calculator adjusts by
      the difference between your cycle length and 28 days.
    </p>

    <h3>Why IVF dates are exact</h3>
    <p>
      In a normal cycle, nobody knows exactly when fertilisation happened. In IVF the clinic does.
      Gestational age is conventionally counted from two weeks before conception, so the calculation
      is transfer date + 280 − 14 − the embryo's age. That gives 261 days after a day-5 blastocyst
      transfer, 263 after a day-3 transfer, and 260 after a day-6 transfer.
    </p>

    <h3>When a scan disagrees with your dates</h3>
    <p>
      A first-trimester scan measures crown-rump length, which varies very little between healthy
      embryos at the same age. That makes it more reliable than a remembered period date. Standard
      obstetric guidance is to re-date the pregnancy when an early ultrasound differs from the LMP
      estimate by more than about five to seven days. After 22 weeks, scan dating becomes much less
      precise because growth rates diverge, and the earlier estimate is usually kept.
    </p>

    <h2>Understanding gestational age</h2>
    <p>
      Gestational age counts from the first day of your last period, about two weeks before
      conception actually occurred. It is a historical convention from when the period date was the
      only knowable landmark, and it is why you are already described as "four weeks pregnant" when a
      test first turns positive, despite the embryo being about two weeks old.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ages are written as weeks + days, so "12+4" means twelve weeks and four days. Fetal age
      (post-conception age) is always two weeks less than gestational age.
    </p>

    <h2>Trimesters</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Trimester</th>
            <th scope="col">Weeks</th>
            <th scope="col">What is going on</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>First</td>
            <td>0+0 to 13+6</td>
            <td>All major organ systems form. Nausea and fatigue peak around weeks 8–10 and usually ease by 14.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Second</td>
            <td>14+0 to 27+6</td>
            <td>The anatomy scan, first felt movements, and for most people the most comfortable stretch.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Third</td>
            <td>28+0 to birth</td>
            <td>Rapid fetal growth and weight gain, more frequent monitoring, and preparation for birth.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Term definitions</h2>
    <p>
      The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists replaced the single "term" label with
      four categories in 2013, because outcomes differ measurably across them:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Label</th>
            <th scope="col">Gestational age</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Preterm</td><td>Before 37+0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Early term</td><td>37+0 to 38+6</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Full term</td><td>39+0 to 40+6</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Late term</td><td>41+0 to 41+6</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Post term</td><td>42+0 and beyond</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>How likely is the due date?</h2>
    <p>
      Only about 4 percent of babies are born on their estimated date, and roughly 80 percent arrive
      within the two weeks either side of it. For a first pregnancy the most common arrival point is
      several days <em>after</em> 40 weeks; subsequent pregnancies tend to run slightly shorter. The
      due date is best understood as the middle of a distribution, not a deadline.
    </p>

    <h2>What this calculator cannot do</h2>
    <p>
      It works from dates alone. It cannot account for irregular cycles you have not measured,
      multiple pregnancies (twins are usually delivered earlier), medical conditions affecting
      gestation length, or a planned induction or caesarean. Your midwife or doctor will set the date
      your care actually follows, and if they have re-dated you after a scan, use their number.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
